Both Oriel and Tauri can build AI desktop apps: a web UI, a native backend,
typed calls between them. The difference is where the model runs. **In Oriel,
llama.cpp and whisper.cpp are modules compiled into your executable**, and
tokens reach the page over the same IPC bridge as every other command. **In
Tauri, local models usually run in a second process** (a bundled sidecar such
as `llama-server` or Ollama) or come from Rust crates you wire up yourself.

This page explains what that means in practice, and is explicit about what
Oriel does *not* do yet. [GhostPen](https://highercomve.github.io/GhostPen/)
was ported from Tauri to Oriel, and its README
[measures the two](https://github.com/highercomve/GhostPen#compared-with-the-rust-tauri-ghostpen).

# At a glance

| | Oriel | Tauri |
|---|---|---|
| Local LLM | llama.cpp compiled into the app: `-Dllama` | A sidecar process (`llama-server`, Ollama), or Rust crates (`llama-cpp-2`, candle) |
| Speech to text | whisper.cpp compiled in: `-Dwhisper`, plus `audio_capture` for mic and system audio | A sidecar or crates, plus an audio plugin or crate |
| GPU | Build flags: `-Dggml_cuda`, `-Dggml_vulkan`, Metal on by default on macOS; CPU fallback at runtime | Per sidecar build, or per crate's Cargo features and toolchain on each OS |
| Vision models | `-Dllama_mtmd` (llama.cpp's multimodal library) | Depends on the sidecar or crate |
| JSON output | `oriel.llama.jsonSchemaToGrammar`: JSON Schema to a GBNF grammar | Depends on the sidecar or crate |
| Token streaming | An async command emits typed events; they reach the page before the command's reply | Channels or events from Rust; a UI that fetches from a local HTTP port meets CORS/CSP |
| Processes at runtime | One | Two or more with a sidecar |
| MCP server | ❌ not built in (see MCP below) | ❌ not built in; community plugins and bridges |

# Local inference

## Oriel: one build, one binary

Turn the modules on in `build.zig` (or pass `-Dllama -Dwhisper` to `oriel build`):

```zig
const oriel = b.dependency("oriel", .{
    .target = target,
    .optimize = optimize,
    .llama = true,
    .whisper = true,
});
```

Zig compiles llama.cpp, whisper.cpp and one shared copy of ggml as part of
your app's build: no CMake, no separate download, no second process to start,
supervise or ship. The first build takes about 45 seconds; cached builds don't
recompile them. Your commands call the C API directly (`oriel.llama.c`,
`oriel.whisper.c`), in the same address space as the rest of the app, so there
is no HTTP or socket hop between the UI's command and the model.

GPU acceleration is a build flag, the same on every machine that builds it:

| Flag | Where | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `-Dggml_cuda` | Linux | NVIDIA; CPU fallback when no GPU |
| `-Dggml_vulkan` | Linux, Windows | Any vendor; loaded at runtime, so the app still starts on the CPU without a Vulkan driver |
| Metal | macOS | On by default; kernels embedded, no Xcode `metal` step |

Measured in GhostPen (GTX 1070 Max-Q vs i7-8750H): Qwen3.5 2B Q4_K_M
generates 55 tokens/s on Vulkan vs 15 on the CPU. See
[Modules: local AI inference]($link.page('docs/modules'))
for the API.

## Tauri: a sidecar, or Rust crates

Tauri apps usually take one of two routes:

1. **A sidecar.** Bundle a prebuilt executable (`llama-server`, Ollama, or
   your own) with `bundle.externalBin`, start it from the app, and talk to it
   over HTTP. That is a second process to package for every target triple,
   start, health-check, and stop, with a local port the UI or Rust code
   connects to.
2. **Rust crates.** Bindings such as `llama-cpp-2` build llama.cpp through
   CMake from `build.rs`; candle is a native Rust ML framework. Both work, but
   GPU support is enabled per crate through Cargo features and needs the
   matching toolchain (CUDA, Vulkan SDK, Metal) set up on each build machine.

Neither is wrong: a sidecar is a good fit when the model server should be
shared or updated separately from the app. Oriel's choice is to make the
in-process route the default and to handle the build for you.

# Streaming tokens to the UI

In Oriel, generation runs as an async command on the worker pool and emits an
event per token. Events are typed on both sides, can be emitted from any
thread, and a command's events reach the page before its reply, so the UI
never sees the final result before the last token.

```zig
pub const Events = struct {
    token: struct { id: u32, text: []const u8 },
};
const events = oriel.App.events(Events);

pub const Commands = struct {
    pub const async_commands = .{"generate"};

    pub fn generate(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, args: struct { id: u32, prompt: []const u8 }) ![]const u8 {
        var out: std.ArrayList(u8) = .empty;
        // Your sampling loop over oriel.llama.c (llama_decode, llama_sampler_sample, ...).
        var gen = try Generator.init(args.prompt);
        defer gen.deinit();
        while (try gen.next()) |piece| {
            events.emit(.token, .{ .id = args.id, .text = piece });
            try out.appendSlice(gpa, piece);
        }
        return out.items;
    }
};
```

```ts
import { invoke, listen } from "./oriel";

const id = nextId++;
listen("token", (t) => { if (t.id === id) append(t.text); });
const full = await invoke("generate", { id, prompt });
```

There is no local HTTP server in the path, so there is nothing for CORS or
the page's CSP to block. In Tauri the in-process equivalent is a command that
streams over a `Channel`, which works well; the friction comes with the
sidecar route, where a page that `fetch`es `http://localhost:11434` directly
runs into CORS and the CSP, and apps add the HTTP plugin or a Rust proxy
command to relay the stream.

# Structured output

`oriel.llama.jsonSchemaToGrammar` turns a JSON Schema into a GBNF grammar
with llama.cpp's own converter. Pass it to
`c.llama_sampler_init_grammar(vocab, grammar, "root")` and the model can only
produce JSON that matches the schema, which you then parse with `std.json`
into a Zig struct and return to the page as a typed value.

# Speech and audio

`-Dwhisper` adds whisper.cpp, and the `audio_capture` module records the
microphone or system audio as 16 kHz mono `f32`, the format whisper expects,
on Linux (PulseAudio/PipeWire), Windows and macOS. The ghostpen-lite example
runs live captions this way, on CUDA; GhostPen adds dictation.

# MCP

The [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) lets AI clients
(Claude, Cursor and others) call tools an app exposes. **Oriel has no MCP
module today**, and neither does Tauri: Tauri apps use community projects
(such as `tauri-plugin-mcp-bridge` and `mcp-server-tauri`), which typically
pair a plugin inside the app with a separate MCP server process that talks to
it over a local socket.

What you can do in Oriel now: MCP's stdio transport is newline-delimited
JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout, and `std.json` parses and writes it. An Oriel
app is a single native executable whose `main` is yours, so it can check for
a flag such as `--mcp` before starting the window and serve tools backed by
the same Zig functions as its `Commands`, including llama.cpp, with no Node or
Python bridge. The AI client starts that process itself, so it is separate
from a window the user already has open; to act on the running app, forward
requests to it (for example over a local socket). A Rust app can do the same
with an MCP crate; it is a pattern, not a framework feature, in either case.

# Which to choose

- **Oriel** fits apps that run models on the user's machine and want them in
  the app: one binary, GPU backends chosen at build time, tokens streamed
  over typed events, speech and audio capture in the same build.
- **Tauri** fits apps that talk to a hosted API (OpenAI, Anthropic) or to a
  model server the user already runs, and teams already invested in Rust and
  its ecosystem. For a hosted API, both frameworks work the same way: a
  command makes the HTTPS request and streams the response to the page.
